  "result": "All 8 tests pass.\n\n**What changed** in `ratelimit/limiter.py` (the existing sliding-window-log design was kept):\n\n- **Factored out eviction** into a private helper so both `allow` and `remaining` drop expired timestamps the same way.\n- **`remaining(key, now)`** evicts expired entries and returns the effective limit minus the live entries, floored at zero. It uses a plain lookup so querying an unseen key does not create an entry.\n- **`reset(key)`** removes the key's log entirely rather than clearing it, so forgotten keys do not leak memory.\n- **`set_limit(key, limit)`** stores a per-key override in a separate dict. `allow` and `remaining` consult the override first and fall back to the constructor limit. It validates the limit is at least 1, matching the constructor.\n\nOne judgment call: `reset` clears usage but leaves a `set_limit` override in place, since the override is configuration rather than state. That is documented in the docstring and is easy to flip if you want reset to wipe the override too.",
